WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.736
One bright ticket unfolds into a small
event.

00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:09.137
A high price stands beside a short queue,
while empty seats wait beyond the

00:00:09.137 --> 00:00:09.522
entrance.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.616
The ticket price slides downward and the
queue becomes longer.

00:00:19.616 --> 00:00:24.772
More people enter, but every ticket now
brings in less money than before.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.635
Price and quantity multiply into a revenue
ribbon.

00:00:34.635 --> 00:00:41.110
It rises, bends, and eventually falls,
showing that extra sales do not always

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.106
increase receipts.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.335
A second ribbon arrives for costs: the
venue, each extra visitor, and growing

00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:53.939
service demands. Money received is only
part of the decision.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.252
Watch the gap between the revenue and cost
ribbons.

00:01:04.252 --> 00:01:09.856
It can widen, then narrow, even while the
number of tickets sold keeps rising.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.946
Before opening the doors, the event needs
a price.

00:01:18.946 --> 00:01:21.876
Does selling more always mean earning more
profit?

00:01:21.876 --> 00:01:24.439
Build the functions and test that claim.
